mmc-pwrseq-simple is the only thing that powers the Zero 2W's radio: one
`reset-gpios` entry on PG18 holding WL_REG_ON in reset, and the RTC's 32 kHz
fanout as its sleep clock. On 6.18 that node no longer probes.

A single `reset-gpios` is taken as a reset controller reference first:
devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared() is asked for it, and only when that
finds nothing does the driver fall back to the GPIO array at the bottom of the
same probe. Finding nothing is exactly the case here — the node has no `resets`
— but the reset core's GPIO fallback declines with -ENOENT before it ever looks
for a controller:

	/* Currently only #gpio-cells=2 is supported with the meaning of:
	 * args[0]: GPIO number
	 * args[1]: GPIO flags
	 */
	if (args->args_count != 2)
		return -ENOENT;

sunxi's pio is #gpio-cells = <3> (bank, pin, flags), so the fallback refuses,
the refusal is returned as if it were a real error rather than as "no reset",
and the probe ends there:

	pwrseq_simple wifi-pwrseq: error -ENOENT: reset control not ready
	pwrseq_simple wifi-pwrseq: probe with driver pwrseq_simple failed with error -2

What follows is mmc1 never probing — it is waiting on a pwrseq device that
failed — so the SDIO bus is empty, the UWE5622 is never powered, and the board
comes up with no wlan0 and therefore no access point.

Treat -ENOENT as what it is: no reset controller for this node, so use the GPIO
array the driver already has. Every board whose radio reset is a plain GPIO and
whose GPIO controller uses three cells is in the same position, which is why
this is a patch here and not a device tree change.

--- a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
@@ -137,9 +137,17 @@
 	ngpio = of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "reset-gpios", "#gpio-cells");
 	if (ngpio == 1) {
 		pwrseq->reset_ctrl = devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(dev, NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
-			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
-					     "reset control not ready\n");
+		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl)) {
+			/*
+			 * -ENOENT is the node having no `resets` and no reset
+			 * provider the GPIO fallback can build one from. The
+			 * GPIO array below is the sequence then.
+			 */
+			if (PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl) != -ENOENT)
+				return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
+						     "reset control not ready\n");
+			pwrseq->reset_ctrl = NULL;
+		}
 	}
 
 	/*
